Within the easiest scoto-seesaw framework, we analyze the simplest variant of type-I 2HDM, where the presence of a light scalar singlet, $0.4\, {\rm TeV}
\lesssim m_{s_1}\lesssim 5\, {\rm TeV}$, is indicated by experimental data. This model effectively describes Weakly-Interacting Massive Particles (WIMPs) in the range $0.4\, {\rm TeV}
\lesssim m_{\chi}\lesssim 5\, {\rm TeV}$, with its dark matter relic density meeting the requirements of the easiest scoto-seesaw framework. Consequently, this model's dark matter direct detection is characterized by the standard "WIMP paradigm" signature, with a Compton scattering dominant cross-section. In addition, we investigate the stability of the vacuum in the easiest scoto-seesaw framework, finding that it is robust against electroweak precession for $m_{s_1} \gtrsim 1.3\, {\rm TeV}$. Furthermore, we examine the interplay between dark matter direct detection and Higgs vacuum stability, finding that the latter imposes a more stringent constraint on the parameter space.
